ries has only three yearly points, so it says little about where 27% sits in a range.\n- **Skew:** It's flat, so you aren't paying extra for downside or upside strikes.\n- **Term structure:** It's nearly flat and slightly upward. IV is 26% for Jan-2027, 27% for Jan-2028 and 27.5% for Jan-2029. Longer expiries don't carry much of a premium.\n\nSo the options price doesn't give a reason to add or to hold off. Whether to add depends on your view of VLTX itself, such as grid-equipment demand and the utilities' ability to finance capex. Other nodes in this run are looking at those questions. If you do add, the Jan-2028 expiry is a reasonable choice. Its IV is close to the median, and it gives more time than Jan-2027 for the thesis to play out without the Jan-2029 premium.\n\nThis covers implied volatility only. I didn't have premiums, strikes, spot price or realized volatility, so I couldn't compare IV with realized volatility or check specific contracts.","ttft_ms":2013,"type":"result","duration_ms":21370,"uuid":"52a6e45f-62f3-43a6-9cae-f33d572b4d54","ttft_stream_ms":1119,"time_to_request_ms":126,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":6,"system_prompt":9,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":11,"input_attachments":29,"input_hooks":3,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":7,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":11,"init_frame_send":8,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":6,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":4,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":16,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":2,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":3,"request_build":5},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":21,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":63,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":175,"process_anon_mib_turn_start":100,"process_rss_mib_result":195,"process_anon_mib_result":113,"process_peak_rss_mib":195,"process_cpu_ms_turn":559,"process_wall_ms_turn":21369,"process_cpu_ms_streaming":136,"process_wall_ms_streami